{"term":"moral","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:56dd8630a14682e3e6","definition":"Of or relating to principles of right and wrong in behaviour, especially conforming to principles of rectitude.","example":"moral judgments; a moral poem","pos":"adj","synonyms":["ethical","good","decent","righteous"],"antonyms":["immoral","unethical","corrupt","unscrupulous","amoral","non-moral","pseudomoral","unmoral"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moral#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:f5bfadfe82a25cfafa","definition":"Conforming to a given standard of right behaviour.","example":"a moral action","pos":"adj","synonyms":["acceptable","appropriate","courteous","decent","decorous","mannerly","proper","incorruptible","noble","righteous","virtuous"],"antonyms":["immoral","unethical","corrupt","unscrupulous","amoral","non-moral","pseudomoral","unmoral"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moral#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ce4bb37bfeb425250f","definition":"Being sanctioned by or operative on one's conscience or ethical judgment.","example":"The stories did not seem to me to touch life. They were plainly intended to have a bracing moral effect, and perhaps had this result for the people at whom they were aimed. They left me with the impression of a well-delivered stereopticon lecture, with characters about as life-like as the shadows on the screen, and whisking on and off, at the mercy of the operator.","pos":"adj","synonyms":[],"antonyms":["immoral","unethical","corrupt","unscrupulous","amoral","non-moral","pseudomoral","unmoral"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribu